A||||2||fiero56||still eating belts(alt alignment)||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||05-26-2001||02:14 PM||||I replaced the water pump on my '86SE 2.8 the other day. The input shaft on the pump was slightly bent, and I assumed this was the cause for belts to be eaten up after only a few miles of driving. Well, I decided to take the car out this morning for its first voyage with the new pump(down to 87ECO5's place, but no-one was there). On the way back, while driving back roads(about 20 miles on the new belt and pump), the belt starts squealing and it slings off the inside rib, as it always does. The new water pump didn't fix the problem. I now have my sights set on the alternator. It appears to my eye to be set in toward the engine about 1/8", relative to crank and w/p pulleys. This would run the belt off center of the other pulleys and eat the inside rib off, like it is doing. <P>Has anyone ever experienced this before? The alt braket is good, no cracks, it is bolted properly. I've been looking the whole thing over, and can't figure out how I would set the alt outwards a little. Any ideas?||||reg||5
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||05-26-2001||02:49 PM||||Had that problem on a old van I had before..   Cure?   I spaced the alt. out the correct lenth with a washer behind it. <P>Might work for your problem also by using a washer to line it better.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||05-26-2001||03:46 PM||||I finally figured it out. I parked the SE right next to my Formula, raised both decklids and just compared everything about the pulley system. Finally, after a lot of looking and comparing, I noticed that the alt pulley on the SE is designed for a 4 rib belt(the crank and w/p pulleys are for 5), and on my Formula all pulleys are 5 rib belt pulleys. This means, somewhere along the way, somebody probably needed to replace the alternator and instead of buying a new one, pulled one from a GM in salvage yard, and never payed attention to belt systems. I have a spare pulley I will use for now, hopefully this fixes the problem(I've said that before).||||reg||10
